\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {1}{\ignorespaces The changed response and paired market decisions. \textbf  {(A)}~The same four neighboring intentions produce different responses at the trader marked $i$: three Buys and one Hold give neighbor sum 1, producing Buy under Rule~19280 and Sell under Rule~19286. The hollow center's current intention is not an input. Every arrangement with the same neighbor sum produces the same response. \textbf  {(B)}~Complete lookup tables, with the baseline and switched rules aligned by neighbor sum. The highlighted column is the only response changed by the switch. Blue, gray, and orange denote Buy~(0), Hold~(1), and Sell~(2); arrows indicate rule evaluation or a changed response, not completed trades. \textbf  {(C)}~Paired $64\times 64$ grids at the rule switch and after 100, 400, and 3,000 steps. The upper row retains the baseline rule; the lower assigns the new rule to 20\% of interior traders. Both markets share a 1,000-step baseline, dealer depth $h=100$, and active technical traders. Starting market 14 was selected from 50 by proximity to the median paired difference in peak Sell fraction. Ensemble outcomes and conditional onset times appear in Supplementary Table~\ref {tab:paired-switch}.}}{21}{figure.caption.31}\protected@file@percent }
\providecommand*\caption@xref[2]{\@setref\relax\@undefined{#1}}
\newlabel{fig:rule-quench}{{1}{21}{The changed response and paired market decisions. \textbf {(A)}~The same four neighboring intentions produce different responses at the trader marked $i$: three Buys and one Hold give neighbor sum 1, producing Buy under Rule~19280 and Sell under Rule~19286. The hollow center's current intention is not an input. Every arrangement with the same neighbor sum produces the same response. \textbf {(B)}~Complete lookup tables, with the baseline and switched rules aligned by neighbor sum. The highlighted column is the only response changed by the switch. Blue, gray, and orange denote Buy~(0), Hold~(1), and Sell~(2); arrows indicate rule evaluation or a changed response, not completed trades. \textbf {(C)}~Paired $64\times 64$ grids at the rule switch and after 100, 400, and 3,000 steps. The upper row retains the baseline rule; the lower assigns the new rule to 20\% of interior traders. Both markets share a 1,000-step baseline, dealer depth $h=100$, and active technical traders. Starting market 14 was selected from 50 by proximity to the median paired difference in peak Sell fraction. Ensemble outcomes and conditional onset times appear in Supplementary Table~\ref {tab:paired-switch}}{figure.caption.31}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {2}{\ignorespaces Rule switches across dealer depths over 3,000 post-switch steps. \textbf  {(A)}~Probability of an interior Sell majority with technical traders held, or active at dealer depths $h=0.1$, 1, and 100. Bands are 95\% Wilson intervals over 50 starting markets. \textbf  {(B)}~Active-minus-held majority probability at each depth and fraction. \textbf  {(C)}~Median maximum drawdown at fraction $f=0.20$, with interquartile bands. \textbf  {(D)}~Median fraction of post-switch eligible orders rejected at fraction $f=0.20$, on a logarithmic axis with interquartile bands. Dealer depth $h$ is the dealer's initial shares per trader. Because the dealer's cash and share reserves keep a constant product, $h$ also sets how far the quoted price moves for a given net trade. At zero dealer depth only opposing trader orders match and price stays fixed. Panels C and D describe switched markets. No-switch control drawdowns and paired differences appear in Supplementary Table~\ref {tab:paired-switch}.}}{22}{figure.caption.32}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:reservoir}{{2}{22}{Rule switches across dealer depths over 3,000 post-switch steps. \textbf {(A)}~Probability of an interior Sell majority with technical traders held, or active at dealer depths $h=0.1$, 1, and 100. Bands are 95\% Wilson intervals over 50 starting markets. \textbf {(B)}~Active-minus-held majority probability at each depth and fraction. \textbf {(C)}~Median maximum drawdown at fraction $f=0.20$, with interquartile bands. \textbf {(D)}~Median fraction of post-switch eligible orders rejected at fraction $f=0.20$, on a logarithmic axis with interquartile bands. Dealer depth $h$ is the dealer's initial shares per trader. Because the dealer's cash and share reserves keep a constant product, $h$ also sets how far the quoted price moves for a given net trade. At zero dealer depth only opposing trader orders match and price stays fixed. Panels C and D describe switched markets. No-switch control drawdowns and paired differences appear in Supplementary Table~\ref {tab:paired-switch}}{figure.caption.32}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3}{\ignorespaces Market size and the formation of a Sell majority over 50 starting markets and 3,000 post-switch steps. \textbf  {(A)}~Majority probability with technical traders held, with 95\% Wilson bands. \textbf  {(B)}~Susceptibility with technical traders held: the variance across markets of the final 500-step mean Sell fraction, multiplied by the number of interior traders. \textbf  {(C)}~Interpolated fraction $f_{50}(L)$ at which half the markets reached a majority, against inverse grid size, with technical traders held at dealer depth $h=100$ and active at $h=1$ and $h=100$. \textbf  {(D)}~Sampled maximum susceptibility versus grid size. Error bars in C and D are 95\% whole-market bootstrap intervals. Solid lines connect tested values; dashed curves in A are logistic fits. No law for indefinitely large grids was fitted.}}{23}{figure.caption.33}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:finite-size}{{3}{23}{Market size and the formation of a Sell majority over 50 starting markets and 3,000 post-switch steps. \textbf {(A)}~Majority probability with technical traders held, with 95\% Wilson bands. \textbf {(B)}~Susceptibility with technical traders held: the variance across markets of the final 500-step mean Sell fraction, multiplied by the number of interior traders. \textbf {(C)}~Interpolated fraction $f_{50}(L)$ at which half the markets reached a majority, against inverse grid size, with technical traders held at dealer depth $h=100$ and active at $h=1$ and $h=100$. \textbf {(D)}~Sampled maximum susceptibility versus grid size. Error bars in C and D are 95\% whole-market bootstrap intervals. Solid lines connect tested values; dashed curves in A are logistic fits. No law for indefinitely large grids was fitted}{figure.caption.33}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {4}{\ignorespaces Five example markets extended to 10,000 steps with active technical traders and dealer depth $h=100$. Blue, gray, and orange denote Buy, Hold, and Sell. \textbf  {(A)}~Grid decisions at the rule switch and after 1,500, 3,000, 5,000, and 10,000 steps. The examples were selected from the 3,000-step summaries before any images were inspected. At that endpoint, the first two markets had not reached a majority and the other three had. Nonmajority examples were nearest their group's median final Sell fraction; majority examples were nearest their group's median onset, with the starting-market number breaking ties. Starting markets were 2, 15, 41, 5, and 38, from left to right. The longer outcomes did not affect selection. Because each trader's update ignores its own intention, the two checkerboard halves of the grid can hold different intentions, which appears as regions where Sell and Hold alternate from cell to cell; no such extended regions appear in the example markets with revision probability $r=0.9$ (Supplementary Fig.~\ref {fig:variants-panels}). \textbf  {(B)}~Quoted price divided by its value at the rule switch. \textbf  {(C)}~Interior Sell fraction, with one-half marked by a horizontal line. The dark vertical dashed line marks the original 3,000-step endpoint; other guides mark the remaining snapshots. Onsets and financial summaries appear in Supplementary Table~\ref {tab:market-panel-horizons}.}}{24}{figure.caption.34}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:dose-panels}{{4}{24}{Five example markets extended to 10,000 steps with active technical traders and dealer depth $h=100$. Blue, gray, and orange denote Buy, Hold, and Sell. \textbf {(A)}~Grid decisions at the rule switch and after 1,500, 3,000, 5,000, and 10,000 steps. The examples were selected from the 3,000-step summaries before any images were inspected. At that endpoint, the first two markets had not reached a majority and the other three had. Nonmajority examples were nearest their group's median final Sell fraction; majority examples were nearest their group's median onset, with the starting-market number breaking ties. Starting markets were 2, 15, 41, 5, and 38, from left to right. The longer outcomes did not affect selection. Because each trader's update ignores its own intention, the two checkerboard halves of the grid can hold different intentions, which appears as regions where Sell and Hold alternate from cell to cell; no such extended regions appear in the example markets with revision probability $r=0.9$ (Supplementary Fig.~\ref {fig:variants-panels}). \textbf {(B)}~Quoted price divided by its value at the rule switch. \textbf {(C)}~Interior Sell fraction, with one-half marked by a horizontal line. The dark vertical dashed line marks the original 3,000-step endpoint; other guides mark the remaining snapshots. Onsets and financial summaries appear in Supplementary Table~\ref {tab:market-panel-horizons}}{figure.caption.34}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {5}{\ignorespaces The same starting market at different dealer depths with fraction $f=0.20$. \textbf  {(A)}~Grid decisions at the rule switch and after 500, 1,500, and 3,000 steps. The first three columns have active technical traders at dealer depth $h=0.1$, $1$, and $100$; the last holds the technical traders at $h=100$. Starting market 2 was selected among markets reaching a majority at all three dealer depths with active technical traders by minimizing the summed distance from their depth-specific median onsets, with the starting-market number breaking ties. \textbf  {(B)}~Quoted price divided by its value at the rule switch. \textbf  {(C)}~Interior Sell fraction. With technical traders held, no decision responds to the quoted price, while interior traders still trade and the quoted price still updates. The four runs share the same starting market; ensemble probabilities and drawdowns appear in Fig.~\ref {fig:reservoir}.}}{25}{figure.caption.35}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:dealer-panels}{{5}{25}{The same starting market at different dealer depths with fraction $f=0.20$. \textbf {(A)}~Grid decisions at the rule switch and after 500, 1,500, and 3,000 steps. The first three columns have active technical traders at dealer depth $h=0.1$, $1$, and $100$; the last holds the technical traders at $h=100$. Starting market 2 was selected among markets reaching a majority at all three dealer depths with active technical traders by minimizing the summed distance from their depth-specific median onsets, with the starting-market number breaking ties. \textbf {(B)}~Quoted price divided by its value at the rule switch. \textbf {(C)}~Interior Sell fraction. With technical traders held, no decision responds to the quoted price, while interior traders still trade and the quoted price still updates. The four runs share the same starting market; ensemble probabilities and drawdowns appear in Fig.~\ref {fig:reservoir}}{figure.caption.35}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {6}{\ignorespaces Changing trader cash and shares at the rule switch, with active technical traders, fraction $f=0.18$, and 50 starting markets over 3,000 post-switch steps. In A--C, the nine resource combinations are grouped by cash multiplier, with share multiplier on the axis and a series for each dealer depth. \textbf  {(A)}~Majority probability. The dashed line and band give the held-condition probability and its 95\% Wilson interval, identical across resource combinations and dealer depths. \textbf  {(B)}~Median maximum drawdown of switched markets on a logarithmic axis; this comparison had no no-switch controls. \textbf  {(C)}~Median fraction of post-switch eligible orders rejected, on a logarithmic axis. \textbf  {(D)}~Majority probability against median drawdown for all 27 active combinations, with the held reference repeated. The drawdown axis follows B. Probability axes cover the full possible range; all displayed drawdowns and rejection medians are strictly positive.}}{26}{figure.caption.36}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:balance-sheet}{{6}{26}{Changing trader cash and shares at the rule switch, with active technical traders, fraction $f=0.18$, and 50 starting markets over 3,000 post-switch steps. In A--C, the nine resource combinations are grouped by cash multiplier, with share multiplier on the axis and a series for each dealer depth. \textbf {(A)}~Majority probability. The dashed line and band give the held-condition probability and its 95\% Wilson interval, identical across resource combinations and dealer depths. \textbf {(B)}~Median maximum drawdown of switched markets on a logarithmic axis; this comparison had no no-switch controls. \textbf {(C)}~Median fraction of post-switch eligible orders rejected, on a logarithmic axis. \textbf {(D)}~Majority probability against median drawdown for all 27 active combinations, with the held reference repeated. The drawdown axis follows B. Probability axes cover the full possible range; all displayed drawdowns and rejection medians are strictly positive}{figure.caption.36}{}}
