\@writefile{lot}{\contentsline {table}{\numberline {S1}{\ignorespaces Design of the four main comparisons. Active technical traders responded to the quoted price; held technical traders stayed in Hold. The grid-size comparison used active technical traders at dealer depth $h=1$ and $100$, and held technical traders at $h=100$. Counts include the no-switch controls. Where a comparison tested several fractions, one control per starting market and condition served all of them. The cash-and-shares comparison had no no-switch controls.}}{34}{table.caption.108}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{tab:campaigns}{{S1}{34}{Design of the four main comparisons. Active technical traders responded to the quoted price; held technical traders stayed in Hold. The grid-size comparison used active technical traders at dealer depth $h=1$ and $100$, and held technical traders at $h=100$. Counts include the no-switch controls. Where a comparison tested several fractions, one control per starting market and condition served all of them. The cash-and-shares comparison had no no-switch controls}{table.caption.108}{}}
\@writefile{lot}{\contentsline {table}{\numberline {S2}{\ignorespaces Paired rule switches on a $64\times 64$ grid over 3,000 post-switch steps. In A, majority probability $p$ is the fraction of markets reaching an interior Sell fraction of at least one-half; median onset is conditional on reaching that value. Undefined means no run reached a majority. In B, DD is median maximum drawdown in percent. $\Delta $DD is the median paired difference, in percentage points, with a pointwise 95\% whole-market bootstrap confidence interval (CI). These contrasts separate the rule switch from baseline financial variation.}}{34}{table.caption.109}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{tab:paired-switch}{{S2}{34}{Paired rule switches on a $64\times 64$ grid over 3,000 post-switch steps. In A, majority probability $p$ is the fraction of markets reaching an interior Sell fraction of at least one-half; median onset is conditional on reaching that value. Undefined means no run reached a majority. In B, DD is median maximum drawdown in percent. $\Delta $DD is the median paired difference, in percentage points, with a pointwise 95\% whole-market bootstrap confidence interval (CI). These contrasts separate the rule switch from baseline financial variation}{table.caption.109}{}}
\@writefile{lot}{\contentsline {table}{\numberline {S3}{\ignorespaces Grid-size comparisons over 50 starting markets and 3,000 post-switch steps. Brackets give 95\% confidence intervals (CI) from 2,000 whole-market bootstrap samples. $n_{\rm  int}$ counts tested fractions with majority probability strictly between 0.05 and 0.95. The fractions $f_{25}$, $f_{50}$, and $f_{75}$ are the first upward crossings of majority probability 0.25, 0.5, and 0.75, found by linear interpolation between tested fractions, and the width is $f_{75}-f_{25}$. The susceptibility maximum is taken over the tested fractions and is selected again in each bootstrap sample.}}{35}{table.caption.110}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{tab:finite-size-scaling}{{S3}{35}{Grid-size comparisons over 50 starting markets and 3,000 post-switch steps. Brackets give 95\% confidence intervals (CI) from 2,000 whole-market bootstrap samples. $n_{\rm int}$ counts tested fractions with majority probability strictly between 0.05 and 0.95. The fractions $f_{25}$, $f_{50}$, and $f_{75}$ are the first upward crossings of majority probability 0.25, 0.5, and 0.75, found by linear interpolation between tested fractions, and the width is $f_{75}-f_{25}$. The susceptibility maximum is taken over the tested fractions and is selected again in each bootstrap sample}{table.caption.110}{}}
\@writefile{lot}{\contentsline {table}{\numberline {S4}{\ignorespaces Probability of a Sell majority after changes in trader cash and shares over 50 starting markets and 3,000 post-switch steps. Each fraction includes 27 combinations of cash, shares, and dealer depth. With technical traders held, the probability was the same across those combinations; with technical traders active, it spanned the stated range.}}{35}{table.caption.111}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{tab:balance-fractions}{{S4}{35}{Probability of a Sell majority after changes in trader cash and shares over 50 starting markets and 3,000 post-switch steps. Each fraction includes 27 combinations of cash, shares, and dealer depth. With technical traders held, the probability was the same across those combinations; with technical traders active, it spanned the stated range}{table.caption.111}{}}
\@writefile{lot}{\contentsline {table}{\numberline {S5}{\ignorespaces Majority outcomes with technical traders active and held, paired by starting market, over 50 starting markets and 3,000 post-switch steps. Fractions 0.17 and 0.18 come from the unchanged-resource runs of the trader-resource comparison, which reproduced the dealer-depth runs exactly where the two overlapped. Only active and Only held count markets that reached a majority under one condition alone; held technical traders stayed in Hold from initialization, so paired markets need not share their state at the switch. The difference is active minus held majority probability, with a 95\% interval (CI) from 2,000 bootstrap samples of whole starting markets. Average rows weight the listed fractions equally and count market--fraction pairs.}}{36}{table.caption.112}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{tab:feedback-pairs}{{S5}{36}{Majority outcomes with technical traders active and held, paired by starting market, over 50 starting markets and 3,000 post-switch steps. Fractions 0.17 and 0.18 come from the unchanged-resource runs of the trader-resource comparison, which reproduced the dealer-depth runs exactly where the two overlapped. Only active and Only held count markets that reached a majority under one condition alone; held technical traders stayed in Hold from initialization, so paired markets need not share their state at the switch. The difference is active minus held majority probability, with a 95\% interval (CI) from 2,000 bootstrap samples of whole starting markets. Average rows weight the listed fractions equally and count market--fraction pairs}{table.caption.112}{}}
\@writefile{lot}{\contentsline {table}{\numberline {S6}{\ignorespaces A one-time instruction to sell versus a persistent rule switch at the same selected interior traders. Both conditions used dealer depth $h=100$, 3,000 post-intervention steps, and no-switch controls. Entries are majority probabilities with 95\% Wilson intervals.}}{36}{table.caption.113}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{tab:revision-pulse}{{S6}{36}{A one-time instruction to sell versus a persistent rule switch at the same selected interior traders. Both conditions used dealer depth $h=100$, 3,000 post-intervention steps, and no-switch controls. Entries are majority probabilities with 95\% Wilson intervals}{table.caption.113}{}}
\@writefile{lot}{\contentsline {table}{\numberline {S7}{\ignorespaces All single-response changes to Rule~19280 with dealer depth $h=100$. Each entry gives majority probability with technical traders held / active over 20 starting markets and 3,000 post-switch steps. Outputs are Buy $=0$, Hold $=1$, and Sell $=2$. This descriptive screen used no multiplicity adjustment.}}{37}{table.caption.114}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{tab:revision-rules}{{S7}{37}{All single-response changes to Rule~19280 with dealer depth $h=100$. Each entry gives majority probability with technical traders held / active over 20 starting markets and 3,000 post-switch steps. Outputs are Buy $=0$, Hold $=1$, and Sell $=2$. This descriptive screen used no multiplicity adjustment}{table.caption.114}{}}
\@writefile{lot}{\contentsline {table}{\numberline {S8}{\ignorespaces Adaptive versus frozen band widths for active technical traders at fraction $f=0.175$ over 50 starting markets. Frozen widths kept $\sigma _t$ at its value immediately before the rule switch, while the reference price $\mu _t$ continued to update. Entries $p$ are Sell-majority probabilities with 95\% Wilson confidence intervals (CI) in brackets. Sell is the mean fraction of technical traders in Sell over post-switch steps and starting markets. The probability at dealer depth $h=0.1$ minus that at $h=100$ was -0.06 with adaptive widths and -0.26 with frozen widths. Their paired difference was 0.20, with a 95\% interval [-0.08, 0.50] from 2,000 whole-market bootstrap samples.}}{37}{table.caption.115}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{tab:revision-band}{{S8}{37}{Adaptive versus frozen band widths for active technical traders at fraction $f=0.175$ over 50 starting markets. Frozen widths kept $\sigma _t$ at its value immediately before the rule switch, while the reference price $\mu _t$ continued to update. Entries $p$ are Sell-majority probabilities with 95\% Wilson confidence intervals (CI) in brackets. Sell is the mean fraction of technical traders in Sell over post-switch steps and starting markets. The probability at dealer depth $h=0.1$ minus that at $h=100$ was -0.06 with adaptive widths and -0.26 with frozen widths. Their paired difference was 0.20, with a 95\% interval [-0.08, 0.50] from 2,000 whole-market bootstrap samples}{table.caption.115}{}}
\@writefile{lot}{\contentsline {table}{\numberline {S9}{\ignorespaces Additional dealer-depth comparisons at fraction $f=0.20$ with active technical traders and 50 starting markets over 3,000 post-switch steps. Because the dealer's cash and share reserves keep a constant product, the initial slope of the log quoted price against net purchases per trader is approximately $2/h$; the model has no separate price-impact coefficient. Depth changes funding and price sensitivity together. Drawdown is a percentage, and the dealer-rejection fraction uses post-switch eligible orders. Probability brackets are 95\% Wilson intervals.}}{37}{table.caption.116}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{tab:revision-impact}{{S9}{37}{Additional dealer-depth comparisons at fraction $f=0.20$ with active technical traders and 50 starting markets over 3,000 post-switch steps. Because the dealer's cash and share reserves keep a constant product, the initial slope of the log quoted price against net purchases per trader is approximately $2/h$; the model has no separate price-impact coefficient. Depth changes funding and price sensitivity together. Drawdown is a percentage, and the dealer-rejection fraction uses post-switch eligible orders. Probability brackets are 95\% Wilson intervals}{table.caption.116}{}}
\@writefile{lot}{\contentsline {table}{\numberline {S10}{\ignorespaces Observation period and the fraction $f_{50}$ at which half the markets reached a Sell majority, with technical traders held and dealer depth $h=100$. Entries are interpolated $f_{50}$ with 95\% whole-market bootstrap intervals over 50 starting markets. Criterion columns give the interior Sell fraction a run had to reach to count as a majority. These runs used one fixed set of tested fractions rather than the adaptively refined set, so interpolated midpoints can differ slightly from those in Supplementary Table~\ref {tab:finite-size-scaling} for the same runs, for example 0.167 rather than 0.166 at $L=128$ and 0.219 rather than 0.216 at $L=32$.}}{38}{table.caption.117}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{tab:revision-horizons}{{S10}{38}{Observation period and the fraction $f_{50}$ at which half the markets reached a Sell majority, with technical traders held and dealer depth $h=100$. Entries are interpolated $f_{50}$ with 95\% whole-market bootstrap intervals over 50 starting markets. Criterion columns give the interior Sell fraction a run had to reach to count as a majority. These runs used one fixed set of tested fractions rather than the adaptively refined set, so interpolated midpoints can differ slightly from those in Supplementary Table~\ref {tab:finite-size-scaling} for the same runs, for example 0.167 rather than 0.166 at $L=128$ and 0.219 rather than 0.216 at $L=32$}{table.caption.117}{}}
\@writefile{lot}{\contentsline {table}{\numberline {S11}{\ignorespaces Logistic fits and susceptibility maxima over 50 starting markets and 3,000 post-switch steps. Parentheses give whole-market bootstrap standard errors (SE). The fitted curve imposes increasing probability; interpolated crossings do not. The final column gives the tested fraction with the largest susceptibility, so its precision is limited by the spacing of nearby tested fractions.}}{38}{table.caption.118}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{tab:revision-fits}{{S11}{38}{Logistic fits and susceptibility maxima over 50 starting markets and 3,000 post-switch steps. Parentheses give whole-market bootstrap standard errors (SE). The fitted curve imposes increasing probability; interpolated crossings do not. The final column gives the tested fraction with the largest susceptibility, so its precision is limited by the spacing of nearby tested fractions}{table.caption.118}{}}
\@writefile{lot}{\contentsline {table}{\numberline {S12}{\ignorespaces The five example markets of Fig.~\ref {fig:dose-panels} and all 50 starting markets at fraction $f=0.15$ with their no-switch controls, extended from 3,000 to 10,000 steps with dealer depth $h=100$ and active technical traders. Times are steps after the rule switch, following 1,000 baseline steps. Drawdowns and price changes are percentages; price changes use the quoted price at the rule switch as reference. Final Sell averages the last 500 steps of the longer observation period. None means no Sell majority by 10,000 steps. The examples were selected from the 3,000-step summaries before any images were inspected. The extensions follow the same starting markets, and are not independent replications.}}{39}{table.caption.119}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{tab:market-panel-horizons}{{S12}{39}{The five example markets of Fig.~\ref {fig:dose-panels} and all 50 starting markets at fraction $f=0.15$ with their no-switch controls, extended from 3,000 to 10,000 steps with dealer depth $h=100$ and active technical traders. Times are steps after the rule switch, following 1,000 baseline steps. Drawdowns and price changes are percentages; price changes use the quoted price at the rule switch as reference. Final Sell averages the last 500 steps of the longer observation period. None means no Sell majority by 10,000 steps. The examples were selected from the 3,000-step summaries before any images were inspected. The extensions follow the same starting markets, and are not independent replications}{table.caption.119}{}}
\@writefile{lot}{\contentsline {table}{\numberline {S13}{\ignorespaces Transaction-fee comparisons. Each condition used 50 starting markets. The fee was charged to each completed buyer and seller from initialization. Sell-majority probabilities are followed by Wilson 95\% intervals. In Panel A, drawdown and cumulative dealer fees are medians for the switched runs at fraction $f=0.20$. Drawdown uses the 3,000-step post-switch observation period, including the quoted price immediately before the switch. Fees include all 4,000 trading steps and are expressed as a percentage of initial aggregate trader cash. Fee income was held separately from trading reserves. Panel B reports paired differences between 25 and zero basis points with percentile 95\% intervals from 2,000 whole-market bootstrap samples. The probability difference is a difference in proportions; drawdown differences are median paired changes in percentage points. The complete protocol, all six tested fractions, and paired differences are archived with the transaction-fee study.}}{40}{table.caption.120}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{tab:transaction-fees}{{S13}{40}{Transaction-fee comparisons. Each condition used 50 starting markets. The fee was charged to each completed buyer and seller from initialization. Sell-majority probabilities are followed by Wilson 95\% intervals. In Panel A, drawdown and cumulative dealer fees are medians for the switched runs at fraction $f=0.20$. Drawdown uses the 3,000-step post-switch observation period, including the quoted price immediately before the switch. Fees include all 4,000 trading steps and are expressed as a percentage of initial aggregate trader cash. Fee income was held separately from trading reserves. Panel B reports paired differences between 25 and zero basis points with percentile 95\% intervals from 2,000 whole-market bootstrap samples. The probability difference is a difference in proportions; drawdown differences are median paired changes in percentage points. The complete protocol, all six tested fractions, and paired differences are archived with the transaction-fee study}{table.caption.120}{}}
\@writefile{lot}{\contentsline {table}{\numberline {S14}{\ignorespaces Following experiments on $64\times 64$ grids in which every interior trader kept Rule~19280, with 1,000 baseline steps and 3,000 steps of following. Followers were interior traders chosen at random. Cash is the mean over followers of each follower's final cash, after every trader sold all of its shares to the dealer, divided by its cash and shares at the quoted price when following began, minus the same mean for the same traders in the paired no-following control. Non-follower Sell is the change in the fraction of time that non-followers spent in Sell, and drawdown is the change in the maximum drawdown of the quoted price. All changes are in percentage points, with pointwise 95\% intervals from resampling whole starting markets. In A, each starting market had two random assignments of followers and advice. In the masked condition, neighbors read each follower's intention from the no-following control, and the cash difference is advice minus masked. The two endpoints fixed before these markets were simulated, cash with advice and the cash difference at dealer depth $h=100$ with technical traders held, had Bonferroni-adjusted 97.5\% intervals of [25.43, 28.06] and [22.30, 25.30]. In the no-following controls at $h=100$ with technical traders held, the same non-followers spent 24.9\% of their time in Sell. In B, the change in the final 500 steps after advice stopped exceeded 20 percentage points in 38 of 100 runs and was below 5 percentage points in 61. In C, copying repeated a randomly chosen trader's completed trade from the previous step.}}{41}{table.caption.121}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{tab:following}{{S14}{41}{Following experiments on $64\times 64$ grids in which every interior trader kept Rule~19280, with 1,000 baseline steps and 3,000 steps of following. Followers were interior traders chosen at random. Cash is the mean over followers of each follower's final cash, after every trader sold all of its shares to the dealer, divided by its cash and shares at the quoted price when following began, minus the same mean for the same traders in the paired no-following control. Non-follower Sell is the change in the fraction of time that non-followers spent in Sell, and drawdown is the change in the maximum drawdown of the quoted price. All changes are in percentage points, with pointwise 95\% intervals from resampling whole starting markets. In A, each starting market had two random assignments of followers and advice. In the masked condition, neighbors read each follower's intention from the no-following control, and the cash difference is advice minus masked. The two endpoints fixed before these markets were simulated, cash with advice and the cash difference at dealer depth $h=100$ with technical traders held, had Bonferroni-adjusted 97.5\% intervals of [25.43, 28.06] and [22.30, 25.30]. In the no-following controls at $h=100$ with technical traders held, the same non-followers spent 24.9\% of their time in Sell. In B, the change in the final 500 steps after advice stopped exceeded 20 percentage points in 38 of 100 runs and was below 5 percentage points in 61. In C, copying repeated a randomly chosen trader's completed trade from the previous step}{table.caption.121}{}}
\@writefile{lot}{\contentsline {table}{\numberline {S15}{\ignorespaces Revision timing. At each step, each interior trader adopted its rule's output with revision probability $r$ and otherwise kept its current intention; $r=1$ is the main model. Entries are the interpolated fraction $f_{50}$ at which half of 50 starting markets reached a Sell majority within 3,000 post-switch steps, with 95\% intervals in brackets from 2,000 bootstrap samples of whole starting markets, drawn once and shared by all conditions. Active $-$ held is the difference in $f_{50}$ at dealer depth $h=100$, from the same samples. Halves is the largest median, over the three tested fractions nearest the held $f_{50}$, of the absolute difference between the Sell fractions of the two checkerboard halves of the grid, averaged over five snapshots in the last 400 steps. Panel B uses technical traders held at dealer depth $h=100$ and revision probability $r=0.9$; the width is $f_{75}-f_{25}$. No no-switch control reached a Sell majority in any condition.}}{42}{table.caption.122}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{tab:variants-timing}{{S15}{42}{Revision timing. At each step, each interior trader adopted its rule's output with revision probability $r$ and otherwise kept its current intention; $r=1$ is the main model. Entries are the interpolated fraction $f_{50}$ at which half of 50 starting markets reached a Sell majority within 3,000 post-switch steps, with 95\% intervals in brackets from 2,000 bootstrap samples of whole starting markets, drawn once and shared by all conditions. Active $-$ held is the difference in $f_{50}$ at dealer depth $h=100$, from the same samples. Halves is the largest median, over the three tested fractions nearest the held $f_{50}$, of the absolute difference between the Sell fractions of the two checkerboard halves of the grid, averaged over five snapshots in the last 400 steps. Panel B uses technical traders held at dealer depth $h=100$ and revision probability $r=0.9$; the width is $f_{75}-f_{25}$. No no-switch control reached a Sell majority in any condition}{table.caption.122}{}}
\@writefile{lot}{\contentsline {table}{\numberline {S16}{\ignorespaces Technical traders inside the grid, with revision probability $r=1$. In addition to the technical traders along the edge, band-rule technical traders occupied a fraction $\rho $ of interior positions, excluding the traders given the new rule; the row of 63 positions adds as many interior technical traders as there are along the edge. Reach is the mean fraction of the other interior traders with at least one technical trader among their four neighbors. Entries are interpolated $f_{50}$ over 50 starting markets and 3,000 post-switch steps, with 95\% intervals in brackets from 2,000 bootstrap samples of whole starting markets shared by all conditions; active $-$ held is the difference in $f_{50}$ at dealer depth $h=100$. Panel B uses interior fraction $\rho =0.05$. At grid size $L=128$ with technical traders held, majority probability rose steeply enough that only three tested fractions had probabilities strictly between 0.05 and 0.95. No no-switch control reached a Sell majority in any condition.}}{42}{table.caption.123}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{tab:variants-placement}{{S16}{42}{Technical traders inside the grid, with revision probability $r=1$. In addition to the technical traders along the edge, band-rule technical traders occupied a fraction $\rho $ of interior positions, excluding the traders given the new rule; the row of 63 positions adds as many interior technical traders as there are along the edge. Reach is the mean fraction of the other interior traders with at least one technical trader among their four neighbors. Entries are interpolated $f_{50}$ over 50 starting markets and 3,000 post-switch steps, with 95\% intervals in brackets from 2,000 bootstrap samples of whole starting markets shared by all conditions; active $-$ held is the difference in $f_{50}$ at dealer depth $h=100$. Panel B uses interior fraction $\rho =0.05$. At grid size $L=128$ with technical traders held, majority probability rose steeply enough that only three tested fractions had probabilities strictly between 0.05 and 0.95. No no-switch control reached a Sell majority in any condition}{table.caption.123}{}}
\@writefile{lot}{\contentsline {table}{\numberline {S17}{\ignorespaces Inventory-conditioned signals at fraction $f=0.20$ over 50 new starting markets and 3,000 post-intervention steps. Original observations passed every intention; inventory-conditioned observations passed Hold instead of Sell from a sender with no shares. Each mode had a switched branch and a no-switch control with identical pre-intervention histories. Entries are branch means, except majority entries, which count the markets reaching a raw Sell fraction of one-half. Late Sell fraction averages the final 500 steps. The final column is the inventory-conditioned switch effect minus the original switch effect, with a percentile 95\% interval from 10,000 bootstrap samples of whole starting markets (Eq.~\ref {eq:visibility-contrast}). Differences in Panels A--C are percentage points; Panel D uses completed sales per interior trader. The primary outcome was Panel A with technical traders held and dealer depth $h=100$; other comparisons are descriptive.}}{43}{table.caption.124}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{tab:visibility}{{S17}{43}{Inventory-conditioned signals at fraction $f=0.20$ over 50 new starting markets and 3,000 post-intervention steps. Original observations passed every intention; inventory-conditioned observations passed Hold instead of Sell from a sender with no shares. Each mode had a switched branch and a no-switch control with identical pre-intervention histories. Entries are branch means, except majority entries, which count the markets reaching a raw Sell fraction of one-half. Late Sell fraction averages the final 500 steps. The final column is the inventory-conditioned switch effect minus the original switch effect, with a percentile 95\% interval from 10,000 bootstrap samples of whole starting markets (Eq.~\ref {eq:visibility-contrast}). Differences in Panels A--C are percentage points; Panel D uses completed sales per interior trader. The primary outcome was Panel A with technical traders held and dealer depth $h=100$; other comparisons are descriptive}{table.caption.124}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {S1}{\ignorespaces Five example markets with revision probability $r=0.9$, active technical traders, and dealer depth $h=100$ over 3,000 post-switch steps, the counterpart of Fig.~\ref {fig:dose-panels} without the separate checkerboard halves of the main model. Blue, gray, and orange denote Buy, Hold, and Sell. The fractions $f=0.235$, 0.300, 0.310, and 0.335 were the tested fractions whose majority probabilities at revision probability $r=0.9$ (0.00, 0.62, 0.84, and 1.00) were nearest those of the fractions in Fig.~\ref {fig:dose-panels} at $r=1$ (0.00, 0.50, 0.84, and 1.00), with ties broken by proximity to the $r=0.9$ midpoint. Within each fraction and outcome, the market nearest the median onset, or the median final Sell fraction for markets without a majority, was selected from the campaign summaries before any images were inspected, with the starting-market number breaking ties. \textbf  {(A)}~Grid decisions at the rule switch and after 500, 1,500, and 3,000 steps. Starting markets were 5, 29, 38, 18, and 8, from left to right. \textbf  {(B)}~Quoted price divided by its value at the rule switch. \textbf  {(C)}~Interior Sell fraction, with one-half marked by a horizontal line. Midpoints and widths appear in Supplementary Table~\ref {tab:variants-timing}.}}{44}{figure.caption.125}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:variants-panels}{{S1}{44}{Five example markets with revision probability $r=0.9$, active technical traders, and dealer depth $h=100$ over 3,000 post-switch steps, the counterpart of Fig.~\ref {fig:dose-panels} without the separate checkerboard halves of the main model. Blue, gray, and orange denote Buy, Hold, and Sell. The fractions $f=0.235$, 0.300, 0.310, and 0.335 were the tested fractions whose majority probabilities at revision probability $r=0.9$ (0.00, 0.62, 0.84, and 1.00) were nearest those of the fractions in Fig.~\ref {fig:dose-panels} at $r=1$ (0.00, 0.50, 0.84, and 1.00), with ties broken by proximity to the $r=0.9$ midpoint. Within each fraction and outcome, the market nearest the median onset, or the median final Sell fraction for markets without a majority, was selected from the campaign summaries before any images were inspected, with the starting-market number breaking ties. \textbf {(A)}~Grid decisions at the rule switch and after 500, 1,500, and 3,000 steps. Starting markets were 5, 29, 38, 18, and 8, from left to right. \textbf {(B)}~Quoted price divided by its value at the rule switch. \textbf {(C)}~Interior Sell fraction, with one-half marked by a horizontal line. Midpoints and widths appear in Supplementary Table~\ref {tab:variants-timing}}{figure.caption.125}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {S2}{\ignorespaces Inventory information in the primary setting: technical traders held, dealer depth $h=100$, and grid size $L=64$. Black denotes original signals and purple inventory-conditioned signals. Solid lines and points denote switched markets; dashed horizontal lines denote the shared no-switch controls. Each point used the same 50 starting markets and 3,000 post-intervention steps; joining lines guide the eye. \textbf  {(A)}~Probability of reaching a raw Sell majority, with Wilson 95\% intervals. \textbf  {(B)}~Mean raw Sell fraction over the final 500 steps. \textbf  {(C)}~Mean maximum quoted-price drawdown. \textbf  {(D)}~Mean completed sales per interior trader. Bands in B--D are pointwise percentile 95\% intervals from 10,000 bootstrap samples of whole starting markets. The original sweep covered fractions $f=0.10$ to 0.25; larger fractions were added under the prespecified extension rule (Appendix~\ref {app:visibility}). Controls retain Rule~19280 in both observation modes, so the change in control outcomes measures the information intervention alone. The comparison tests one hypothetical response to observing inventory.}}{45}{figure.caption.126}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:visibility}{{S2}{45}{Inventory information in the primary setting: technical traders held, dealer depth $h=100$, and grid size $L=64$. Black denotes original signals and purple inventory-conditioned signals. Solid lines and points denote switched markets; dashed horizontal lines denote the shared no-switch controls. Each point used the same 50 starting markets and 3,000 post-intervention steps; joining lines guide the eye. \textbf {(A)}~Probability of reaching a raw Sell majority, with Wilson 95\% intervals. \textbf {(B)}~Mean raw Sell fraction over the final 500 steps. \textbf {(C)}~Mean maximum quoted-price drawdown. \textbf {(D)}~Mean completed sales per interior trader. Bands in B--D are pointwise percentile 95\% intervals from 10,000 bootstrap samples of whole starting markets. The original sweep covered fractions $f=0.10$ to 0.25; larger fractions were added under the prespecified extension rule (Appendix~\ref {app:visibility}). Controls retain Rule~19280 in both observation modes, so the change in control outcomes measures the information intervention alone. The comparison tests one hypothetical response to observing inventory}{figure.caption.126}{}}
